The code you posted should work fine. You can also try:

```
Parameters params = cam.getParameters();
params.setFlashMode("torch");
cam.setParameters(params);
```

Also make sure your device supports torch mode. You can check this by calling `getSupportedFlashModes()` method of `Parameters` class.

**Edit:** I think you need to add `FLASHLIGHT` permission to your manifest file.

The problem is that you're not saving the tensor with the same data type as it was loaded from.

```
// Save tensor with float32 data type
torch::Tensor x_sequence = torch::linspace(0, M_PI, 1000).to(torch::kFloat32);
torch::save(x_sequence, "x_sequence.dat");

// Load tensor with float64 data type
torch::Tensor x_read;
torch::load(x_read, "x_sequence.dat");
std::cout << "Data type of x_read: " << x_read.scalar_type() << std::endl;

// This will fail because the data types don't match
assert(x_read.is_same(x_sequence));
```

To fix this, you can either save the tensor with the desired data type:

```
torch::Tensor x_sequence = torch::linspace(0, M_PI, 1000).to(torch::kFloat64);
```

Or load the tensor with the correct data type:

```
torch::Tensor x_read;
torch::load(x_read, "x_sequence.dat").to(torch::kFloat64);
```

The problem is that you're not copying the data from `dataview` into `mFloatArray`. You just create an array of the same length and then do nothing with it.

Change this:

```
for (var i = 0; i < mFloatArray.length; i++)
{
    mFloatArray[i] = dataview.getFloat32(i * 4); // At every 4th byte
}
```

to this:

```
mFloatArray.set(new Float32Array(dataview.buffer));
```

The problem is that you're calling `yield` inside a loop. This will cause your function to pause for one second on each iteration of the loop, and then continue immediately after the loop finishes.

If you want to wait for one second between each movement, you should call `yield` outside of the loop:

```
function OnMouseDown() {
    for (var count : int = 0; count &lt; 10; count++) {
        door.transform.position = Vector3(0,0.1,0);
        torch.transform.position = Vector3(0,0.1,0);
        flame.transform.position = Vector3(0,0.1,0);
    }

    yield WaitForSeconds(1.0);
}
```

The output you posted is from [detectron2](https://github.com/facebookresearch/detectron2) training code, which is a fork of [pytorch/faster-rcnn](https://github.com/pytorch/vision/tree/master/references/detection). The meaning of each field can be found in the comments at the top of the file.

```
